Work all milestones in ONE turn; checkpoint STATUS.md as you go; end the turn only when the report is written or you are blocked.

## Read first
AGENTS.md, docs/spike-spec.md, `.coord/reports/T-001.md`, docs/research/third-party.md (GPUI section). Kit 0.7.3 pins GPUI snapshot 0.3.9; keep the lockfile and default backend. Prereqs per Kit install guide: Rust 1.92+, MSVC target, VS Build Tools with C++ and Windows SDK, CMake on PATH (CMake 4.4.4 and VS 2026 are present; the manager installs Rust before you start. Do not install system software yourself).

## Order of work (stop early and report if a gate fails)
1. Verify the toolchain builds a hello-window with the pinned versions. Record build time and any VS2026-vs-VS2022 issues.
2. Gate 1, input probe: log pixel-pan/scroll events with phases while the editor is focused; check the restored Windows source (not upstream main) for the exact phases. Determine whether finger lift is distinguishable from inertia end. Ask the user to do the two-finger pan when ready (exact instructions in STATUS.md "Waiting on user"). If not, try ONE bounded native adapter and report its scope.
3. Then the rest of docs/spike-spec.md: carousel (direct manipulation, release by distance+velocity, spring-back, rubber-band overscroll creating a new note, blank-note pruning), tray + global hotkey toggle of a resident frameless window, transparent/Mica/blur, decorated markdown with visible markers, clickable `- [ ]` in wrapped text (undo/caret stable), Tab/Shift+Tab indent, fenced python highlight, autosave/restore, folding + line numbers probes, measurements (cold launch to editable, hotkey to visible+focused, hide time, idle RAM; scripted where possible).
4. About 10-15 smoke checks, disposable, header `# spike`.

## Rules
- Local git only; commit your work in `spikes/B` and `docs/spikes/B` when done. Do not touch spikes/A.
- Missing tools are blockers: record, don't install.
- REPORT.md: pass/fail per spec item, numbers, screenshots (docs/spikes/B/), effort estimate, blockers, and a plain-language verdict: would you build the real app on this?
# Spike spec (identical for A and B)

Build a disposable prototype in `spikes/<A|B>/`. Report in `docs/spikes/<A|B>/REPORT.md`: pass/fail per item, numbers, screenshots, effort estimate, and what blocked you.

1. Resident tray app. Global hotkey toggles a frameless window that stays initialized while hidden.
2. Backgrounds: true transparent, Acrylic, Mica. Which work; is text crisp.
3. Fixture note: heading colors by level; bold/italic/strike with visible markers; clickable `- [ ]` toggling the text; Tab/Shift+Tab indent on selection; fenced python block highlighted, rest stays markdown.
4. Carousel (MUST): position bound to the gesture (direct manipulation).
   - Content follows drag 1:1 (mouse drag, precision-touchpad two-finger horizontal pan, horizontal wheel delta).
   - On release distance + velocity decide advance or spring back; momentum then snap.
   - Rubber-band overscroll past newest note; release beyond threshold creates a new note; blank interior notes pruned.
   - Word-wrapped notes; vertical scroll within a note still works; horizontal gesture does not fight text selection.
5. Autosave to disk, restore on relaunch.
6. Wishlist probes (not blockers): code folding, line numbers. yes/no/effort.
7. Measurements (script where possible): cold launch to editable, hotkey to visible+focused, hide time, idle RAM, dev-loop friction.

Testing: about 10-15 smoke checks, disposable, header `# spike`. No exhaustive suites.
